{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Report\n\nTwo agents measured the store write lock held ~12 minutes by a process that had ALREADY\nEXITED. Investigated under royal-weariless-zebrafish, which REFUTED both an fd leak and\n`start=-` defeating liveness.\n\n## Premise verified in current `origin/main` (cc69cc1179)\n\n`src/rebar/_store/lock.py`, `_mkdir_lock_is_stale` (**507**), same-host/same-namespace branch,\nlines **565-572**:\n\n```python\nif not _pid_alive(pid): return True                                               # 565-566\nstamped_start = None if fields[\"start\"] == _STAMP_UNKNOWN else fields[\"start\"]    # 567\ncurrent_start = _process_start_time(pid)                                          # 568\nif stamped_start is not None and current_start is not None and stamped_start != current_start:\n    return True                                     # 569-571  -- recycled-pid discriminator\nreturn False                                        # 572\n```\n\n`_process_start_time` (line **265**) reads **only** `/proc/<pid>/stat` (line 273), returning\n`None` on every `OSError` (275-276). macOS has no `/proc`, so it ALWAYS returns `None`;\n`current_start` at 568 is therefore always `None` there, the line-569 condition is\n**unsatisfiable by construction**, and the branch falls to `return False`.\n\nSo when the holder is dead but its pid NUMBER was recycled by an unrelated live process,\n`_pid_alive` reports \"live\" and the lock is judged fresh. That branch is deliberately exempt\nfrom the 1h `_MKDIR_LOCK_STALE_CEILING_S` backstop (constant at **107**; exemption documented\nin the decision table at **525-531**, \"never on a timer alone\") because a live pid is treated\nas positive proof of liveness. With no start time it is not proof — it is a bare pid-number\ncollision. Net effect on any platform without `/proc`: an **unbounded** wedge. Recycling is\nlive on this host (`kern.maxproc: 16000`, already wrapped past the observed stamp pid).\n\nSecondary, already-bounded wedge: `_reclaim_mkdir_lock` (**575**) removes the owner file\n(**596**) then rmdirs (**600**), both best-effort; a failed rmdir leaves an owner-less dir,\ncleared at the ceiling by the **562** owner-unreadable branch. Asserted by a test here, not\nchanged.\n\n## Direction (conservative, as recorded)\n\nApply `_mkdir_lock_age_exceeds_ceiling` on the live-pid branch **only when `current_start` is\nunavailable** — only when the \"live pid\" verdict is unqualified and so not a liveness proof:\n\n* `current_start` known and DIFFERENT from a known `stamped_start` -> reclaim now (unchanged).\n* `current_start` known and matching -> `False`, never reclaimed on a timer (unchanged;\n  preserves \"never break a live owner on a timer alone\" wherever `/proc` exists).\n* `current_start` is `None` -> unqualified verdict, so fall back to the same 1h ceiling that\n  backstops every other unprovable case here. This is the ONLY behaviour change.\n* `stamped_start` `None` with `current_start` known -> unchanged `False` (not widened).\n\n**Why the ceiling cannot break a genuinely-live, non-recycled owner.** The worry is a real\nowner, alive and holding on a no-`/proc` platform, losing its lock at 1h. The dual-leg\ninvariant rules it out: `_mkdir_lock_is_stale` is only reached from `_acquire_mkdir` (**602**),\nwhich by its stated precondition runs only AFTER this acquirer took the exclusive\n`fcntl.flock` leg, held for the ENTIRE lifetime of the mkdir leg (release order: mkdir, then\nfcntl). A live owner inside its hold still holds that fcntl leg, so a reclaimer could not have\nacquired it and cannot be at this line at all. Reaching the ceiling check is itself evidence\nthe stamped owner let go — the orphan case. Blast radius: orphans only.\n\n**Considered and deliberately NOT done:** also reclaiming on `fcntl_held` here. That same\ninvariant would justify reclaiming an orphan immediately rather than after an hour, but it\nwidens *when a lock may be broken* beyond the recorded direction, so it is left out and noted\nfor a follow-up decision.\n\n## Module-size seam (required)\n\n`src/rebar/_store/lock.py` is **798** lines against the hard cap of **800**\n(`.github/module-size-limit.txt`, CI-enforced), so the fix cannot land without a split. Split\non the existing call-graph seam: extract the owner-identity / stamp / staleness / reclaim\ncluster into a new sibling `src/rebar/_store/lock_owner.py` —\n\n`_STAMP_UNKNOWN`, `_STAMP_V2_PREFIX`, `_BOOT_ID_PATH`, `_PID_NS_PATH`, `_MKDIR_OWNER_FILE`,\n`_MKDIR_LOCK_STALE_CEILING_S`, `_read_boot_id`, `_read_pid_namespace_id`, `_process_start_time`,\n`_host_identity`, `_owner_stamp`, `_parse_v2_stamp`, `_describe_stamped_pid`,\n`_mkdir_lock_age_s`, `_mkdir_lock_age_exceeds_ceiling`, `_pid_alive`, `_legacy_stamp_is_stale`,\n`_mkdir_lock_is_stale`, `_reclaim_mkdir_lock`\n\n— leaving `lock.py` with the fcntl leg, `_acquire_mkdir`, `LockHandle`, and the\n`acquire`/`write_lock` API. The cluster is 270 LOC, so `lock_owner.py` lands near 290 (with\nheader and imports) and `lock.py` near 548: both inside the 200-800 band, no file under 100.\n\nThe dependency runs **one way, `lock.py` -> `lock_owner.py`**, no cycle. It is NOT the case\nthat nothing left behind references the cluster: `_acquire_mkdir` calls `_owner_stamp`,\n`_mkdir_lock_is_stale`, `_reclaim_mkdir_lock`, and the two public reporters that stay —\n`write_lock_is_busy` (**333**) and `describe_lock_holder` (**383**) — reach `_parse_v2_stamp`,\n`_describe_stamped_pid`, `_mkdir_lock_age_s`, `_MKDIR_OWNER_FILE`. All become imports from\n`lock_owner`; nothing in `lock_owner` imports `lock`.\n\n**Migration detail — BOTH reference forms, not just patches.** A sweep of the 4 affected test\nmodules found:\n\n* **12 string-form `monkeypatch.setattr(\"rebar._store.lock.<name>\", …)` sites** over\n  `_read_boot_id`, `_read_pid_namespace_id`, `_process_start_time`, `_pid_alive`,\n  `_parse_v2_stamp`, plus 2 patching `_lock.socket.gethostname` (read by `_host_identity`).\n* **42 plain attribute-access sites** on the `from rebar._store import lock as _lock` binding\n  — the larger half, breaking identically: `_mkdir_lock_is_stale` (17), `_owner_stamp` (13),\n  `_MKDIR_OWNER_FILE` (5), `_reclaim_mkdir_lock`, `_host_identity`,\n  `_MKDIR_LOCK_STALE_CEILING_S` (1 each) — over `test_lock_age_ceiling.py` (14),\n  `test_lock_container_recreate.py` (15), `test_lock_stale_reclaim.py` (9),\n  `test_lock_timeout_names_holder_7084.py` (4).\n\nA re-export shim would keep the attribute half green while **silently breaking the patch\nhalf**: the moved `_mkdir_lock_is_stale` resolves patched names through its OWN module\nglobals, so a shim would leave those tests asserting nothing. No shim — every site of either\nform is repointed at `lock_owner`. No `from rebar._store.lock import <private>` form exists\nanywhere, and no doc cites these by line.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] A dead holder whose pid was recycled by an unrelated live process is reclaimed once past\n      the ceiling where `_process_start_time` returns `None` — test stamps a lock with a\n      live-but-unrelated pid, forces no start time, asserts stale past the ceiling and NOT\n      before it\n- [x] A live holder whose stamped and current start times both exist and MATCH is never\n      reclaimed however old the lock — test asserts refusal well past the ceiling\n- [x] A live pid whose known start time DIFFERS from the stamp is still reclaimed at once,\n      independent of age — test (regression guard on the existing branch)\n- [x] The owner-less-dir path is bounded by the ceiling — test removes the owner file and\n      asserts stale only past it\n- [x] `lock.py` and `lock_owner.py` are each within `.github/module-size-limit.txt` and no new\n      file is under 100 LOC — verified against `git archive HEAD`\n- [x] The split is behaviour-preserving: every pre-existing lock test passes with BOTH\n      reference forms repointed to `lock_owner` (12 patch-string, 42 attribute-access), and\n      `rebar._store.lock` carries no re-export shim for any moved name — test asserts the\n      moved names are absent from `lock`'s namespace"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786564619514198001,"uuid":"d54938fd-d62c-4386-80ff-46cdeff9491a"}